{"task": {"id": "I_kwDOUFmNcc8AAAABUlHmUQ", "item_id": "PVTI_lAHOAHSLSM4BlSCUzg-GxuA", "project_id": "PVT_kwHOAHSLSM4BlSCU", "number": 42, "title": "Showcase: add mobile touch controls to the Web3D metaverse foundation", "body": "# Goal

Make the minimal Web3D metaverse showcase from #40 usable from phones and tablets.

The mobile experience should preserve the same semantic gameplay actions as desktop rather than introducing separate game rules.

## Expected outcome

On a touch device, a user can:

- enter the metaverse showcase;
- move the avatar with a virtual movement control;
- look around by dragging on the screen;
- walk/run with a practical touch interaction;
- jump if the desktop showcase supports jumping;
- trigger the existing world interaction;
- use the experience without requiring a hardware keyboard or mouse.

## Input architecture

Use the existing public semantic input path and `input-experience-extensions` where practical.

Touch input should be adapted into the same semantic actions used by desktop controls.

Do not add mobile-specific movement or interaction logic to the authoritative/game rules merely because the physical input device differs.

## Suggested controls

A reasonable default is:

- left-side virtual joystick: movement;
- right-side drag: camera/look;
- dedicated button: jump;
- dedicated button: interact;
- optional sprint behavior through a button or joystick threshold if it remains understandable on mobile.

The exact visual arrangement may change after testing.

Avoid adding controls that the showcase does not actually need.

## Mobile UI

Touch controls should:

- avoid covering important HUD/world-interaction information;
- use comfortable touch target sizes;
- handle safe-area insets;
- work in common phone and tablet aspect ratios;
- not depend on hover;
- remain understandable without instructions permanently covering the screen.

Landscape may be recommended if it clearly provides the better 3D experience, but the page should fail gracefully in portrait orientation.

## Camera

Touch camera control should:

- feel usable with direct drag input;
- respect existing camera limits and smoothing;
- avoid large jumps when a touch begins or ends;
- not interfere with UI button/joystick touches.

## Performance

Mobile support should not require a new renderer architecture.

If necessary, use bounded mobile defaults such as:

- lower pixel ratio;
- reduced rendering quality;
- reduced optional effects;

but only when actual mobile performance requires them.

Do not introduce speculative performance systems in this Issue.

## Testing

Provide repeatable verification for the touch-input adapter where practical.

At minimum verify:

- movement input is emitted correctly;
- camera drag does not produce invalid values;
- jump/interaction actions are emitted once as intended;
- simultaneous movement and camera input works;
- UI touches do not accidentally trigger world-look input;
- desktop controls continue to work;
- shutdown/disposal removes touch listeners and resources cleanly.

Browser acceptance should include at least one mobile-sized viewport and touch-capable context.

## Relationship to #40

This Issue extends the showcase created in #40.

It should not expand the metaverse feature scope beyond mobile control and presentation support.

In particular, do not introduce multiplayer, Presence, chat, voice, generic replication, or persistent shared-world systems as part of mobile support.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] The #40 metaverse showcase is usable without keyboard or mouse on a touch device.
- [ ] Touch movement drives the same semantic movement path as desktop input.
- [ ] Touch camera look is usable and does not conflict with UI controls.
- [ ] Jump is available on touch if supported by the showcase.
- [ ] The existing world interaction is usable from touch.
- [ ] Touch controls have appropriate mobile sizing and safe-area handling.
- [ ] Desktop keyboard/mouse behavior is not regressed.
- [ ] At least one mobile viewport/touch acceptance path is automated where practical.
- [ ] Touch-related listeners/resources are cleaned up on disposal.
- [ ] The implementation remains narrowly scoped to mobile support for the existing showcase.
